# Carom — Debug and automation surface

Carom carries a small debugging and automation surface, `window.__carom`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or
waiting on real time. It is what reproduces a specific rally, scripts a scenario,
and puts the game into an exact state.

The surface is supplied. `src/debug.ts` holds it, already written, and
`src/main.ts` installs it on `window.__carom` as soon as the engine has
initialized. You neither write it nor install it, and both files are ones you must
not edit.

What this file asks of you is the other half: the game's state must hold what the
surface reads, and the game must honor the state the surface poses. Every
operation is a read or a write of `CaromState`. None of them fabricates an
outcome; each arranges the world, and the game's own `update` — the real
collision, the real serve, the real AI — is what runs from there on the next
frame. Read `src/debug.ts` alongside this file: it is the exact behavior
described here.

Nothing here changes how a person plays. The surface is inert during normal play,
doing nothing until something calls it.

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic, which the physics loop in
`specs/balls.md` already requires: every rate integrated against the delta time
the game is given, so an interval of game time reaches the same state however it
was divided into frames. Two more properties make it driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed
  and must not depend on a canvas, on `requestAnimationFrame`, or on wall-clock
  time to make progress. The dependency runs one way: the simulation never reads
  from, waits on, or is driven by the renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded from
  `state.rngState`, and it keeps its whole generator state in that field, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. A
  build that uses no randomness simply never reads it.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the engine provides instead

The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them at `{{engine.docs}}`. Carom defines none of them, and
`window.__carom` carries no operation for any of them: the engine advances the
game frame by frame and can take it off real time, the engine drives the actions
the game registers, and the engine draws and toggles the debug overlay. What the
overlay shows is Carom's, under [Diagnostics](#diagnostics) below.

## The driver

`state.driver` is how the surface takes the paddles from the player, and honoring
it is the one behavior the whole surface depends on.

- While `driver.paddles` is `false`, the game plays normally: the registered
  actions move the human paddles and, in Solo, the AI moves the right one.
- Every operation except `snapshot` and `reset` sets `driver.paddles` to `true`.
  From then on both paddles move at the held velocities `driver.vy.left` and
  `driver.vy.right`, in px/s, integrated against the frame's delta time and
  clamped to `[PADDLE_MIN_CY, PADDLE_MAX_CY]` exactly as a played paddle is.
  Neither the input actions nor the AI move a paddle while it is `true`.
- The exception is `driver.ai`. In Solo, while `driver.paddles` and `driver.ai`
  are both `true`, the right paddle is driven by the real AI opponent from
  `specs/modes/single-player.md` while the left paddle stays on its held velocity.
- `reset()` sets `driver.paddles` and `driver.ai` back to `false` and both held
  velocities to `0`, handing the paddles back.

A paddle's `vy` field always reports its actual vertical velocity for the frame,
whoever moved it, because that is what the spin mechanic in `specs/balls.md` reads
at contact. A paddle pinned against a bound therefore reports `0` even while a
movement action is held or a driver velocity is set.

## The operations

`window.__carom` carries `version` (`CAROM_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`) and the operations
below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans; coordinates and velocities
are in the logical-pixel space of `specs/overview.md`.

### `reset(options)`

Restores every declared field of `CaromState` to its title-screen value: the title
screen with `menuIndex` at `0`, both scores `0`, no winner, `receiver` back to
`"left"`, `holdTimer` at `0`, both paddles centered at `FIELD_CY`, the ball parked
motionless and spinless at `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)`, an empty trail, `simTime` at
`0`, and the driver cleared. `options.seed` seeds `rngState`, defaulting to
`DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`).

`muted` is deliberately untouched: muting is a player preference the engine owns.

Because it restores exactly the declared fields, any state of your own that a
frame depends on must be derivable from them. Anything authoritative held only in
a field of your own would survive a reset and stop a scenario replaying
identically.

### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under
[Snapshot shape](#snapshot-shape). It never changes anything.

### `startMatch(mode)`

Poses the opening of a real match, with `mode` being `"solo"` or `"versus"`,
exactly as choosing it from the menu would: `screen` becomes `"countdown"`,
`holdTimer` becomes `HOLD_TIME`, both scores `0`, `winner` `null`, `receiver`
`"left"`, both paddles centered, and the ball parked. It does not reset `simTime`.

The game runs from there: the countdown ticks down against the delta time and the
serve happens through the game's own code.

### `serve()`

Ends the pre-serve hold immediately instead of waiting it out, by setting
`holdTimer` to `0`. From a live rally it re-parks the ball, clears the trail, and
returns `screen` to `"countdown"` first, so a re-serve and a first serve behave
identically. It does nothing on any screen other than `"countdown"` or
`"playing"`.

The launch itself is yours: on the next frame the game advances, an elapsed
`holdTimer` launches the ball at `SERVE_SPEED` toward `state.receiver`, exactly as
`specs/balls.md` describes. Nothing else may launch it.

### `setScore(p1, p2)`

Sets the two scores directly, as a precondition. The win and deuce rules still
resolve through real play, so a match ends only when a real point is driven.

### `setPaddle(side, state)`

Poses or moves a paddle. `side` is `"left"` or `"right"`; `state` may set `cy`
(center y) and `vy` (vertical velocity in px/s). A `vy` set here is written to
both the paddle and `driver.vy[side]`, so it persists across frames and the paddle
is still moving when it strikes the ball, which is what drives the spin mechanic.

### `setBall(index, state)`

Places and aims a ball. `index` is `0`, selecting the single ball in play. `state`
may set any of `x`, `y`, `vx`, `vy`, and `spin`.

### `setAiControl(enabled)`

Sets `driver.ai`, handing the right paddle back to the computer opponent for the
rest of the driven scenario, so advancing the game runs the real AI against the
posed ball while the left paddle and the ball stay on their posed values. This is
how a scenario exercises the opponent: pose the ball's approach and the AI
paddle's start, enable it, advance the game, and read where the AI moved its
paddle. It applies only in Solo; `false` is the default, and `reset()` clears it.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "gyre")}}

### `setObstacleClock(t)`

Poses the obstacles by setting the obstacle clock to `t` seconds and holding them
there. `t = 0` is upright at the base centers; a larger `t` sways and rotates them
exactly as normal play would at that moment. While `driver.paddles` is `true` the
obstacle clock is held still rather than advancing with the frame, so a scenario
faces a chosen, known obstacle orientation rather than obstacles sweeping through
the shot. `reset()` returns to normal, moving obstacles.
{{/if}}

A typical scenario calls `startMatch("versus")`, then `setPaddle` and `setBall` to
arrange the exact contact wanted, advances the game a handful of frames through
the engine to run the real collision, and reads the result from `snapshot()`.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "countdown" | "playing" | "paused" | "matchover",
  mode: "solo" | "versus",
  score: { p1: <number>, p2: <number> },
  winner: "left" | "right" | null,   // the winning side once the match is over
  muted: <boolean>,                  // whether the mute toggle is currently on
  paddles: {
    left:  { cy: <number>, vy: <number> },
    right: { cy: <number>, vy: <number> },
  },

  // The single ball in play.
  ball: { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
          speed: <number>, spin: <number>, held: <boolean> },

{{#if (eq variant.slug "gyre")}}
  // Each obstacle's live pose: its center and its rotation (radians), the same
  // values that drive its oriented collision.
  obstacles: [
    { cx: <number>, cy: <number>, theta: <number> },
  ],
{{/if}}
  simTime: <number>,   // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

Every field is read straight off `CaromState`, so what the snapshot reports is
exactly what the game holds. Two are derived rather than stored: `speed` is
`hypot(vx, vy)`, and `held` is true while a ball's pre-serve countdown is still
running, which stays true through a pause.

Three fields therefore need the game to keep them honest every frame:

- `muted` mirrors the engine's mute bit. Refresh it from `api.audio.muted()` in
  every `update`, since the engine owns muting and this is the game's readable
  copy of it.
- `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every frame the match simulation
  advances.

- `holdTimer` counts down from `HOLD_TIME` to `0` and sits at `0` during a live
  rally.

## Diagnostics

The engine's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources, from `InitApi.diagnostics`. Register at least the current
`screen` and `mode`, both scores, each ball's position, velocity, speed, and spin,
and each paddle's center and velocity, the same facts `snapshot()` reports. Keep
each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so
watching the overlay never changes what the simulation does.

Registering those values is the whole of Carom's part. Drawing the panel, toggling
it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
